The `mergeKatexOptions` logic is sound. A double-patch risk was identified: the setter calls `katexLoadedCallback`, which may re-trigger the setter and cause an infinite loop. The plan is to rewrite overrides.js completely and add explicit `\\[` and `\\]` delimiters to the distill layout. A dedicated `processKatexPreambles` helper will extract and render `.katex-preamble` content before the main callback runs.

The implementation will store a shared macros object on `window.__distillKatexMacros` and avoid `defineProperty` entirely. The `renderContent` patch will hardcode this shared object, while the wrapped `katexLoadedCallback` will inject it into `DMath._katexOptions` with `globalGroup: true` before invoking the original callback. This keeps the patch minimal and prevents double-patching.

A capture-phase `DOMContentLoaded` listener in overrides.js will intercept `DMath._katexOptions` before the template initializes. Registration order matters because the template registers its listener first.

The capture-phase DOMContentLoaded listener is too late. The template script registers its handler first and sets katexOptions before overrides.js runs. KaTeX may already be loading by then. The setter or katexLoadedCallback must be patched before DOMContentLoaded fires. The defineProperty approach should work if it is not broken.

A jQuery loading error could crash the overrides IIFE before it patches anything. The revised plan removes `defineProperty` entirely and relies solely on wrapping `katexLoadedCallback` and `DMath.prototype.renderContent`.

Patching `addKatex` is another viable interception point. Since the setter calls `addKatex` when front matter sets `katexOptions`, wrapping that method would merge the shared macros into `_katexOptions` before KaTeX loads. This avoids the need to patch the setter directly.

The `addKatex` wrapper is being implemented now. The user's `\Prob` macro will render a blackboard P. The global macros list needs cleanup because both `\Prob` and `\sP` are defined.

